 \reviewquestion{2.2} Regarding the characterization of SEDAR's comment-and-restore mechanism\textemdash{}if both MORPH and SEDAR ultimately feed compatible seeds to GRIFFIN, what is the exact difference in their post-mutation handling? Do seeds repaired by MORPH produce semantically equivalent mutated queries, whereas SEDAR's approach loses that equivalence? If not, what is the claimed advantage?
 
 \reply
-We have clarified the difference between the two mutation-handling strategies in Section~IV (Evaluation). \tool{} repairs parser-incompatible fragments before mutation, so the seeds fed to \griffin{} are already compatible with both the fuzzer parser and the target DBMS, and no post-mutation handling is needed. \sedar{} instead temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments during mutation and restores them afterward; restored fragments that contain translation errors therefore remain unrepaired and may cause the mutated test case to fail on the target DBMS, as illustrated by the motivating example in Section~II (Background and Motivation). We have also stated explicitly that neither strategy guarantees semantic equivalence of the mutated queries. The advantage of \tool{} is therefore not equivalence after mutation, but that it avoids carrying unrepaired translation errors into mutated test cases. Under controlled conditions with the same 300 translated seeds per target DBMS, \tool{}'s handling achieves 4.0\%--27.2\% higher branch coverage than \sedar{}'s handling. Before mutation, \tool{} also achieves higher result and state equivalence than \sedar{} on the sampled MySQL-to-DuckDB cases (52.0\%/74.0\% vs. 38.0\%/69.0\%). Details can be found in \questionref{1.1}.
+We have clarified in Section~IV (Evaluation) that \tool{} performs pre-migration repair to make seeds parser-compatible, whereas \sedar{} defers some compatibility handling until after mutation. Neither strategy guarantees semantic equivalence after mutation. On 100 sampled MySQL-to-DuckDB test cases, \tool{} achieves higher result and state equivalence than \sedar{} (52.0\%/74.0\% vs. 38.0\%/69.0\%). We have also sampled the same 300 translated seeds and applied the respective mutation handling of \tool{} and \sedar{} with \griffin{} for 24 hours. \tool{} finds 27.2\%, 15.5\%, 4.0\%, and 9.4\% more branches than \sedar{} on DuckDB, MonetDB, Virtuoso, and ClickHouse, respectively.
 
 % Source: Reviewer B, Detailed 1.
 \reviewcomment{2.1} \textbf{Contribution of AQT and compositional baselines.} The authors do not clearly articulate what fundamentally new capability AQT provides over existing SQL representations (e.g., JOOQ's parse tree or SQLGlot's AST) or translation systems (CrackSQL, RISE, QTRAN). More importantly, the evaluation lacks compositional baselines such as ``existing SQL translator + SEDAR/GRIFFIN'' or ``AST-based translation + GRIFFIN''.
